@page "/components/treeview"

<DocsPage>
    <DocsPageHeader Title="Tree View" Component="@nameof(MudTreeView<T>)">
        <Description>
            A highly‑customizable component for visualising hierarchical data with rich selection, lazy‑loading, filtering, and templating features.
        </Description>
    </DocsPageHeader>

    <DocsPageContent>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Basic">
                <Description>
                    <p><strong>MudTreeView</strong> renders items in a parent-child hierarchy. Every <CodeInline Tag>MudTreeViewItem</CodeInline> has a <CodeInline>Text</CodeInline> label and can carry a <CodeInline>Value</CodeInline> of type <CodeInline>T</CodeInline>.</p>
                    <p>The label-value relationship works as follows:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>If <CodeInline>T</CodeInline> is <CodeInline>string</CodeInline> and you omit <CodeInline>Value</CodeInline>, the <CodeInline>Text</CodeInline> is also stored as the value.</li>
                        <li>If you omit <CodeInline>Text</CodeInline> but set <CodeInline>Value</CodeInline>, the component calls <CodeInline>.ToString()</CodeInline> on the value to generate a label.</li>
                        <li>You may provide both to display a label that differs from the stored value—helpful when <CodeInline>T</CodeInline> is a complex type.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>This example sets <CodeInline>ReadOnly="true"</CodeInline>, so the user cannot select items, allowing you to focus on how data is displayed.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewBasicExample)">
                <TreeViewBasicExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Usage">
                <Description>
                    <ul>
                        <li><CodeInline>Hover</CodeInline> - highlight item on pointer‑over.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>Ripple</CodeInline> - show click animation (<em>ignored when</em> <CodeInline>ExpandOnDoubleClick</CodeInline> is true).</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>Dense</CodeInline> - compress vertical padding to fit more rows.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>Disabled</CodeInline> - suppress all user interaction.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>ExpandOnClick</CodeInline> - single‑click expands/collapses a branch.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>ExpandOnDoubleClick</CodeInline> - double‑click expands/collapses and invokes <CodeInline>OnDoubleClick</CodeInline>. This overrides <CodeInline>ExpandOnClick</CodeInline>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewUsageExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewUsageExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Icons">
                <Description>
                    Customise each node with <CodeInline>Icon</CodeInline> and <CodeInline>IconColor</CodeInline>. You can also change the expand/collapse button via <CodeInline>ExpandButtonIcon</CodeInline> and display an alternate icon when the node is open with <CodeInline>IconExpanded</CodeInline>.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewIconExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewIconExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Single Selection">
                <Description>
                    <p>Set <CodeInline>SelectionMode="SelectionMode.SingleSelection"</CodeInline> to allow exactly one selected value in the entire tree. With <CodeInline>SelectionMode.ToggleSelection</CodeInline> the user can click the selected node again to clear the selection.</p>
                    <p>Use <CodeInline>@@bind-SelectedValue</CodeInline> to react to or programmatically change the selection, and adjust the highlight color via <CodeInline>Color</CodeInline>.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewSingleSelectionExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewSingleSelectionExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Multi Selection">
                <Description>
                    <p>With <CodeInline>SelectionMode="SelectionMode.MultiSelection"</CodeInline> users can tick multiple values. Bind <CodeInline>@@bind-SelectedValues</CodeInline> (note the trailing <em>s</em>) to read or control the selection, and change the checkbox colour using <CodeInline>CheckBoxColor</CodeInline>.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewMultiSelectionExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewMultiSelectionExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Binding Items Directly">
                <Description>
                    <p>You may bind <CodeInline>Selected</CodeInline> and <CodeInline>Expanded</CodeInline> directly on individual <CodeInline Tag>MudTreeViewItem</CodeInline> components. This pattern is useful when you generate items with <CodeInline>ItemTemplate</CodeInline> and need fine‑grained control per node.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewBindItemsExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewBindItemsExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Auto‑Expand">
                <Description>
                    <p>When <CodeInline>AutoExpand="true"</CodeInline> a branch that is collapsed automatically expands as soon as a descendant item becomes selected.</p>
                    <p>Programmatically call <CodeInline>ExpandAll()</CodeInline> to open, or <CodeInline>CollapseAll()</CodeInline> to close, every level in the tree.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewAutoExpandExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewAutoExpandExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Item Template">
                <Description>
                    <p>Use <CodeInline>ItemTemplate</CodeInline> to generate nodes from a hierarchical data structure without hand‑coding every item. The template receives your model and can render custom content, attach icons, and propagate the model further down the tree.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewItemTemplateExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewItemTemplateExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Server‑Side Data">
                <Description>
                    <p>Load children on demand with the asynchronous <CodeInline>ServerData</CodeInline> callback. The control will call your function the first time the user expands a node that has no children yet.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Display a custom spinner or icon with <CodeInline>LoadingIcon</CodeInline> and optionally colour it via <CodeInline>LoadingIconColor</CodeInline>.</li>
                        <li>If you already know that a node cannot have children, set <CodeInline>CanExpand="false"</CodeInline> to disable lazy‑loading for that node and avoid an unnecessary network request.</li>
                    </ul>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewServerExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewServerExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Filtering">
                <Description>
                    <p>You can hide nodes that do not match a search term or rule by providing a <CodeInline>FilterFunc</CodeInline>. Call <CodeInline>Filter()</CodeInline> on the <CodeInline>MudTreeView</CodeInline> instance to evaluate the predicate for every node; the helper then sets each node's <CodeInline>Visible</CodeInline> flag.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewFilteringExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewFilteringExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Custom Look & Behaviour">
                <Description>
                    <p>Assign the <CodeInline>Content</CodeInline> fragment on a <CodeInline Tag>MudTreeViewItem</CodeInline> to replace the default layout entirely. This gives you full control over markup, events, and styling.</p>
                    <p>The demo illustrates a non‑standard multi‑selection scheme where selecting a parent does <em>not</em> automatically select its children (and vice‑versa). Only items that satisfy a predicate can be selected.</p>
                    <p>To reproduce the smooth scrolling behaviour, set a fixed <CodeInline>height</CodeInline> or <CodeInline>max‑height</CodeInline> on the surrounding container and enable <CodeInline>overflow‑y: auto</CodeInline>.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Code="@nameof(TreeViewCustomExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewCustomExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Custom Body Content">
                <Description>
                    <p>Use <CodeInline Tag>BodyContent</CodeInline> instead of <CodeInline Tag>Content</CodeInline> if you only need to customise the body of the item but still want the built‑in icons and expander button.</p>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent Outlined="true" Code="@nameof(TreeViewBodyContentExample)" ShowCode="false">
                <TreeViewBodyContentExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

    </DocsPageContent>
</DocsPage>
